   "wikipedia": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just_for_Love",
   "resumo_en": "Just for Love is the fourth album by American psychedelic rock band Quicksilver Messenger Service. Released in August 1970, it marks a stark departure from the extended, blues and jazz-inspired acid rock jamming of their first two albums, to a more traditional pop-rock sound. Dino Valenti, who allegedly discussed forming the group with John Cipollina in 1965 but was apprehended by police on drug charges, finally joined after a stint in prison. Valenti's influence is prevalent throughout the album; he sang every lead and composed eight of its nine tracks under the pseudonym Jesse Oris Farrow. Despite the marked change in the band's sound, it was their third straight album to reach the Top 30 on the Billboard charts, peaking at number 27. The only track from the album released as a single, \"Fresh Air\", became the band's biggest hit, reaching number 49.",
